y=0\\)). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2  \nA sunny line can contain at most two points of \\(T_{3}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume a sunny line contains three distinct points of \\(T_{3}\\).  \nAmong three numbers there are two with the same \\(x\\), or two with the same \\(y\\), or two with the same sum \\(x+y\\) (pigeon\u2011hole principle applied to the three \u201ccolors\u2019\u2019 \\(\\{x\\},\\{y\\},\\{x+y\\}\\)).  \nBy Lemma\u202f1 the line would then be non\u2011sunny, contradicting the assumption. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3  \nThe only unordered pairs of points of \\(T_{3}\\) that can lie on a sunny line are  \n\n\\[\n\\{(1,1),(2,2)\\},\\qquad\n\\{(1,2),(3,1)\\},\\qquad\n\\{(1,3),(2,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nA pair may lie on a sunny line only if the two points have distinct\n\\(x\\)-coordinates, distinct \\(y\\)-coordinates and distinct sums \\(x+y\\) (Lemma\u202f1).  \nChecking all \\(\\binom{6}{2}=15\\) pairs, the three listed above are the only ones satisfying the three distinctness conditions. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Main Argument\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that there exist three distinct lines\n\\(L_{1},L_{2},L_{3}\\) covering all points of \\(T_{3}\\) with exactly two of them sunny.\nWithout loss of generality let \\(L_{1},L_{2}\\) be the sunny lines and \\(L_{3}\\) the non\u2011sunny line.\n\nBy Lemma\u202f2 each sunny line contains at most two points of \\(T_{3}\\); consequently the two sunny lines together cover at most four points.  \nHence at least two points of \\(T_{3}\\) are left to be covered by the non\u2011sunny line \\(L_{3}\\).\n\nBecause of Lemma\u202f3, each sunny line is either one of the three admissible lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{a}: y=x\\;( (1,1),(2,2)),\\qquad\n\\ell_{b}: y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52\\;( (1,2),(3,1)),\\qquad\n\\ell_{c}: y=-2x+5\\;( (1,3),(2,1)),\n\\]\n\nor it contains a single point of \\(T_{3}\\).\n\nWe now examine every possible arrangement.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Case\u202f1 \u2013 Both sunny lines contain a pair.\n\nThere are three sub\u2011cases.\n\n*Sub\u2011case\u202f1a:* \\(\\ell_{a}\\) and \\(\\ell_{b}\\) are sunny.  \nCovered points: \\((1,1),(2,2),(1,2),(3,1)\\).  \nUncovered points: \\((1,3),(2,1)\\).  \nThese two points have different \\(x\\), different \\(y\\) and different sums\n(\\(4\\) and \\(3\\)), so they are not collinear in a horizontal, vertical, or slope \\(-1\\) direction.  \nThus they cannot both lie on the non\u2011sunny line \\(L_{3}\\) \u2013 contradiction.\n\n*Sub\u2011case\u202f1b:* \\(\\ell_{a}\\) and \\(\\ell_{c}\\) are sunny.  \nCovered: \\((1,1),(2,2),(1,3),(2,1)\\).  \nUncovered: \\((1,2),(3,1)\\).  \nAgain the two uncovered points have distinct \\(x\\), distinct \\(y\\) and distinct sums, so they cannot be on a non\u2011sunny line \u2013 contradiction.\n\n*Sub\u2011case\u202f1c:* \\(\\ell_{b}\\) and \\(\\ell_{c}\\) are sunny.  \nCovered: \\((1,2),(3,1),(1,3),(2,1)\\).  \nUncovered: \\((1,1),(2,2)\\).  \nThese two points lie on the line \\(y=x\\), which is sunny, not non\u2011sunny; therefore they cannot be covered by \\(L_{3}\\) \u2013 contradiction.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Case\u202f2 \u2013 One sunny line contains a pair, the other sunny line contains a single point.\n\nWithout loss of generality let the pair be \\(\\{(1,1),(2,2)\\}\\) (the other two pairs are handled analogously).  \nThe second sunny line may be any of the remaining four points of \\(T_{3}\\):\n\\((1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(3,1)\\).  \nFor each choice we list the three points still uncovered and verify that they are not collinear in a non\u2011sunny direction.\n\n| second sunny point \\(P\\) | uncovered points | reason they are not collinear in a non\u2011sunny direction |\n|--------------------------|------------------|--------------------------------------------------------|\n| \\((1,2)\\) | \\((1,3),(2,1),(3,1)\\) | not all share the same \\(x\\), nor the same \\(y\\), nor the same sum |\n| \\((1,3)\\) | \\((1,2),(2,1),(3,1)\\) | same reasoning |\n| \\((2,1)\\) | \\((1,2),(1,3),(3,1)\\) | same reasoning |\n| \\((3,1)\\) | \\((1,2),(1,3),(2,1)\\) | same reasoning |\n\nSince a non\u2011sunny line can only be horizontal, vertical, or of slope \\(-1\\), none of these triples can be covered by a single non\u2011sunny line. Hence this case is impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Case\u202f3 \u2013 Both sunny lines contain only a single point each.\n\nThen at most two points of \\(T_{3}\\) are covered by sunny lines, leaving four points for the non\u2011sunny line \\(L_{3}\\).  \nAny non\u2011sunny line is either horizontal (\\(y=c\\)), vertical (\\(x=c\\)), or of slope \\(-1\\) (\\(x+y=c\\)).  \nInspecting \\(T_{3}\\) we see that each such line contains at most three points of \\(T_{3}\\):\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\ny=1 &: (1,1),(2,1),(3,1)\\\\\ny=2 &: (1,2),(2,2)\\\\\ny=3 &: (1,3)\\\\\nx=1 &: (1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\\\\nx=2 &: (2,1),(2,2)\\\\\nx=3 &: (3,1)\\\\\nx+y=2 &: (1,1)\\\\\nx+y=3 &: (1,2),(2,1)\\\\\nx+y=4 &: (1,3),(2,2),(3,1).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThus no non\u2011sunny line can contain four distinct points of \\(T_{3}\\).  \nConsequently this case is also impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nSince every possible arrangement of exactly two sunny lines leads to a contradiction, such a configuration cannot exist.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For }n=3\\text{, no configuration with }k=2\\text{ sunny lines exists.}}\n\\]"
