            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Configurations with \\(k=0\\)\n\nConsider the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); therefore it lies on the line \\(L_a\\).  \nAll the lines are vertical, hence non‑sunny.  Thus we have a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) with exactly \\(k=0\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Configurations with \\(k=1\\)\n\nTake the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nS:\\;y=x\n\\]\n\n(which has slope \\(1\\)) together with the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=2,3,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(S\\) contains precisely the points \\((a,a)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\); all of them belong to \\(T_n\\).\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) be a point not on \\(S\\); then \\(a\\neq b\\).\n\n*If \\(a>b\\)*, then \\(a\\ge 2\\) and the point lies on the vertical line \\(V_a\\) (since \\(a\\le n\\) by the definition of \\(T_n\\)).\n\n*If \\(a<b\\)*, then \\(b\\ge 2\\). From \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain \\(a\\le n-1\\); consequently the point also lies on the vertical line \\(V_a\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one of the chosen lines.  Exactly one of the lines, namely \\(S\\), is sunny, so we have a configuration with \\(k=1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Non‑existence of configurations with \\(k\\ge 2\\) when \\(n=3\\)\n\nFor \\(n=3\\),\n\n\\[\nT_3=\\{(1,1),\\;(1,2),\\;(1,3),\\;(2,1),\\;(2,2),\\;(3,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nA sunny line cannot be vertical, horizontal or have slope \\(-1\\).  \nAmong the six points, the only pairs that determine a sunny line are \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,2)\\), which give the line \\(y=x\\) (slope \\(1\\)).  \nHence any sunny line can contain at most these two diagonal points.\n\nAssume we have a configuration with two sunny lines.  \nAt most four points of \\(T_3\\) can be covered by the two sunny lines, leaving at least two points uncovered.  \nThe remaining line must be non‑sunny, i.e. vertical, horizontal or of the form \\(x+y=c\\).  \nA direct inspection shows that none of these three types of lines contains both of the uncovered points (the possible candidates are \\(x=1,2,3\\); \\(y=1,2,3\\); \\(x+y=2,3,4\\), and each of them misses at least one point of \\(T_3\\)).  \n\nTherefore no configuration with \\(k\\ge 2\\) sunny lines exists for \\(n=3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Remarks on the general case\n\nThe constructions above settle the existence of configurations for \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\), and the impossibility for \\(k\\ge 2\\) when \\(n=3\\).  \nFor larger \\(n\\) the problem of determining whether values \\(k\\ge 2\\) can be realised remains open; it requires a finer analysis of how many points a sunny line can simultaneously cover and how the remaining points can be distributed among the at most \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines.  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion**\n\n- For every \\(n\\ge 3\\) there exist configurations with exactly \\(k=0\\) sunny lines and with exactly \\(k=1\\) sunny line.  \n- For \\(n=3\\) no configuration with \\(k\\ge 2\\) sunny lines exists.  \n\nThe complete set of admissible values of \\(k\\) for arbitrary \\(n\\) has not been determined in this answer.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
