            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n###  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_{3}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le4\\}\n      =\\{A,B,C,D,E,F\\},\n\\]\n\nwhere  \n\n\\[\nA=(1,1),\\;B=(1,2),\\;C=(1,3),\\;D=(2,1),\\;E=(2,2),\\;F=(3,1).\n\\]\n\nWe must choose three distinct lines \\(L_{1},L_{2},L_{3}\\) such that  \n\n\\[\nT_{3}\\subseteq L_{1}\\cup L_{2}\\cup L_{3},\n\\]\n\nand exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are *sunny* (i.e. not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor the line \\(x+y=0\\)).  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Non‑sunny lines intersecting \\(T_{3}\\)\n\nA non‑sunny line is of one of the three forms  \n\n\\[\nx=c,\\qquad y=c,\\qquad x+y=c .\n\\]\n\nIntersecting each family with \\(T_{3}\\) gives the following eight distinct lines:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&x=1\\;(A,B,C),\\qquad x=2\\;(D,E),\\qquad x=3\\;(F),\\\\\n&y=1\\;(A,D,F),\\qquad y=2\\;(B,E),\\\\\n&x+y=2\\;(B,D),\\qquad x+y=3\\;(C,E),\\qquad x+y=4\\;(F).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Sunny pairs of points\n\nFor any two distinct points of \\(T_{3}\\) compute the slope.  \nThe slope is *sunny* precisely when it is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).  \nA direct computation yields the following admissible pairs:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\{A,E\\}: \\text{slope }1,\\\\\n&\\{B,F\\}: \\text{slope }-\\tfrac12,\\\\\n&\\{C,D\\}: \\text{slope }-2 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nAll other \\(\\binom{6}{2}=15\\) pairs have slope \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\) and therefore cannot be covered by a sunny line.\n\nConsequently a sunny line can contain **at most two** points of \\(T_{3}\\), and the only possible two‑point sunny lines are exactly the three lines described in (2).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Existence for \\(k=0,1,3\\)\n\n*\\(k=0\\).*  \nTake the three non‑sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nL_{1}:x=1,\\qquad L_{2}:y=1,\\qquad L_{3}:x=2 .\n\\]\n\n\\(L_{1}\\) covers \\(A,B,C\\); \\(L_{2}\\) covers \\(A,D,F\\); \\(L_{3}\\) covers \\(D,E\\).  \nHence every point of \\(T_{3}\\) lies on at least one of the three lines, and none of the lines is sunny.  \n\n*\\(k=1\\).*  \nChoose  \n\n\\[\nL_{1}:x=1,\\qquad L_{2}:y=1,\\qquad L_{3}:y=x .\n\\]\n\n\\(L_{1}\\) (vertical) and \\(L_{2}\\) (horizontal) are non‑sunny; \\(L_{3}\\) has slope \\(1\\) and is sunny.  \nA point‑by‑point check:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\n\\text{Point} & \\text{Line containing it}\\\\ \\hline\nA(1,1) & L_{1},L_{2},L_{3}\\\\\nB(1,2) & L_{1}\\\\\nC(1,3) & L_{1}\\\\\nD(2,1) & L_{2}\\\\\nE(2,2) & L_{3}\\\\\nF(3,1) & L_{2}\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nAll six points are covered and exactly one line is sunny.\n\n*\\(k=3\\).*  \nUse the three sunny lines determined by the pairs in (2):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_{1}&:\\;y=x \\quad\\text{through }A,E,\\\\\nL_{2}&:\\;y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac32 \\quad\\text{through }B,F,\\\\\nL_{3}&:\\;y=-2x+5 \\quad\\text{through }C,D .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nTheir slopes are \\(1,-\\tfrac12,-2\\), none of which is \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore all three are sunny.  \nEach line contains exactly the two points indicated, so together they cover the whole set \\(T_{3}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Impossibility for \\(k=2\\)\n\nAssume that a configuration with exactly two sunny lines and one non‑sunny line exists.  \nLet the non‑sunny line be one of the eight lines listed in (1).  \nWe examine each possibility.\n\n| Non‑sunny line | Points covered | Remaining points | Can two sunny lines cover them? |\n|----------------|----------------|------------------|---------------------------------|\n| \\(x=1\\) (covers \\(A,B,C\\)) | \\(A,B,C\\) | \\(D,E,F\\) | No: among \\(\\{D,E,F\\}\\) every pair has slope \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); a sunny line cannot contain any pair. |\n| \\(y=1\\) (covers \\(A,D,F\\)) | \\(A,D,F\\) | \\(B,C,E\\) | No: \\(\\{B,C,E\\}\\) contains only non‑sunny pairs. |\n| \\(x=2\\) (covers \\(D,E\\)) | \\(D,E\\) | \\(A,B,C,F\\) | The only sunny pair among the four is \\(\\{B,F\\}\\); after using it we are left with \\(\\{A,C\\}\\) which is vertical → not sunny. |\n| \\(y=2\\) (covers \\(B,E\\)) | \\(B,E\\) | \\(A,C,D,F\\) | The only sunny pair is \\(\\{C,D\\}\\); the leftover \\(\\{A,F\\}\\) is horizontal → not sunny. |\n| \\(x+y=2\\) (covers \\(B,D\\)) | \\(B,D\\) | \\(A,C,E,F\\) | The only sunny pair is \\(\\{A,E\\}\\); the leftover \\(\\{C,F\\}\\) has slope \\(-1\\) → not sunny. |\n| \\(x+y=3\\) (covers \\(C,E\\)) | \\(C,E\\) | \\(A,B,D,F\\) | The only sunny pair is \\(\\{B,F\\}\\); the leftover \\(\\{A,D\\}\\) is horizontal → not sunny. |\n| \\(x=3\\) (covers \\(F\\)) | \\(F\\) | \\(A,B,C,D,E\\) | Five points cannot be covered by two sunny lines (each sunny line holds at most two points). |\n| \\(x+y=4\\) (covers \\(F\\)) | \\(F\\) | same as previous case | Same impossibility. |\n\nIn every case the three remaining points cannot be partitioned into at most two sunny lines, either because a required pair is non‑sunny or because there are too many points. Hence a configuration with exactly two sunny lines does **not** exist.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Final answer for \\(n=3\\)\n\nThe exhaustive analysis yields\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\in\\{0,\\;1,\\;3\\}}\n\\]\n\nas the complete set of admissible numbers of sunny lines when \\(n=3\\).\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
