            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n###  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \n|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Configurations with \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nEach line of (2) is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence non‑sunny.  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\), therefore \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\).  \nThus the \\(n\\) lines (2) cover all points of \\(T_n\\).  \nConsequently a configuration with **exactly** \\(k=0\\) sunny lines exists for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Configurations with \\(k=1\\)\n\nUse the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-1\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\ntogether with one additional line that passes through the only uncovered point \\((n,1)\\).  \nFor instance the line  \n\n\\[\nL:\\;y=2(x-n)+1\\qquad\\text{(slope }2\\text{)}\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\ncontains \\((n,1)\\) and has slope \\(2\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); hence it is sunny.  \nAll points of \\(T_n\\) lie on one of the lines (3) or on \\(L\\).  \nThus a configuration with exactly one sunny line exists for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  A bound for the number of lattice points on a sunny line  \n\n**Lemma.**  \nLet \\(L\\) be a sunny line. Inside the triangle \\(\\operatorname{conv}(T_n)\\) the line meets the boundary in two distinct sides; consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\#\\bigl(L\\cap T_n\\bigr)\\le s_n:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe three sides of \\(\\operatorname{conv}(T_n)\\) are the lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\qquad y=1,\\qquad x+y=n+1 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(L\\) is not parallel to any of them, it intersects two different sides of (6).  \nBy symmetry we may assume that the intersection points are  \n\n\\[\nP=(1,u),\\qquad Q=(v,1),\n\\]\n\nwith integers \\(u,v\\) satisfying \\(1\\le u,v\\le n\\) and \\(u+v\\le n+2\\) (otherwise the point would lie outside the triangle).  \nThe segment \\(PQ\\) has slope  \n\n\\[\nm=\\frac{1-u}{v-1},\n\\]\n\nwhich is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) because \\(u\\neq1\\), \\(v\\neq1\\) and \\(u+v\\neq n+2\\).  \nAll lattice points of the segment are  \n\n\\[\n(1+t,\\;u-t),\\qquad t=0,1,\\dots ,t_{\\max},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(t_{\\max}= \\min\\{v-1,\\,u-1\\}\\).  \nHence the number of points of \\(T_n\\) on \\(L\\) equals \\(t_{\\max}+1\\).\n\nFrom \\(u+v\\le n+2\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nt_{\\max}= \\min\\{v-1,\\,u-1\\}\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nAdding the initial point gives  \n\n\\[\nt_{\\max}+1\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nThe same estimate holds when the two intersected sides are any other pair among the three sides of (6). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Impossibility of \\(k\\ge 2\\)\n\nAssume that a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the covering condition and contains exactly \\(k\\ge2\\) sunny lines.  \nLet \\(s_n\\) be the bound (5).  \n\n*Each sunny line* contains at most \\(s_n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  \n*Each non‑sunny line* is parallel to one of the three directions in (6); such a line can meet the set \\(T_n\\) in at most \\(n\\) points (for a vertical line the possible \\(x\\)-coordinates are \\(1,\\dots ,n\\), and similarly for the other two directions).  \n\nTherefore the total number of points that can be covered by the whole family is at most  \n\n\\[\nk\\,s_n+(n-k)\\,n .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nUsing \\(s_n\\le\\frac{n+1}{2}\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nk\\,s_n+(n-k)n\n\\le k\\frac{n+1}{2}+(n-k)n\n= n^{2}-\\frac{k}{2}(n-1).\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(k\\ge2\\) and \\(n\\ge3\\),\n\n\\[\nn^{2}-\\frac{k}{2}(n-1)\\;<\\;n^{2}-(n-1)\n\\;<\\;\\frac{n(n+1)}2 = |T_n|\n\\qquad\\text{(by (1)).}\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nThus the right‑hand side of (7) is **strictly smaller** than the number of points that must be covered, a contradiction.  \nConsequently no configuration with \\(k\\ge2\\) sunny lines can exist.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Final answer  \n\nThe constructive part shows that \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are always attainable; the impossibility part shows that no larger \\(k\\) works. Hence for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\)\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\in\\{0,1\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
