nts  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge3\\) define  \n\\[\n   T_n=\\Bigl\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\;\\Big|\\;a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\Bigr\\}.\n\\]\nThe cardinality of \\(T_n\\) is\n\\[\n   |T_n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its direction is not parallel to any of the three\nlines\n\\[\n   \\ell_x:\\;y=0,\\qquad\n   \\ell_y:\\;x=0,\\qquad\n   \\ell_{-1}:\\;x+y=0 .\n\\]\nThus a sunny line has a slope different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Existence of a configuration with \\(k=0\\)\n\nConsider the family of anti\u2011diagonal lines\n\\[\n   L_c:\\;x+y=c\\qquad(c=2,3,\\dots ,n+1).\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\nEach \\(L_c\\) is parallel to \\(\\ell_{-1}\\) and therefore **non\u2011sunny**.\nFor any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(a+b=c\\) for exactly one integer\n\\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line\n\\(L_{a+b}\\).  The \\(n\\) distinct lines \\(\\{L_c\\}_{c=2}^{n+1}\\) therefore\ncover every point of \\(T_n\\) and contain **no** sunny line.  Hence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0\\text{ is attainable for every }n\\ge3 } .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  A bound on the number of lattice points on a sunny line  \n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be a sunny line.  Write its equation in the form\n\\[\n   y=\\frac{p}{q}x+b ,\\qquad p,q\\in\\mathbb Z,\\;q>0,\\;\n   \\gcd(p,q)=1,\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\nwith the additional restrictions \\(p\\neq0\\) (otherwise the line would be\nhorizontal) and \\(p\\neq -q\\) (otherwise the slope would be \\(-1\\)).\nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\cap\\ell\\) then \\(a\\) is an integer and\n\\(b=\\frac{p}{q}a+b_0\\) must also be an integer; hence\n\\(b_0\\in\\frac{1}{q}\\mathbb Z\\) and the set of admissible abscissas is an\narithmetic progression with common difference \\(q\\):\n\\[\n   a\\in\\{a_0,\\,a_0+q,\\,a_0+2q,\\dots\\},\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\nwhere \\(a_0\\) is the smallest integer \\(a\\ge1\\) for which the corresponding\n\\(b\\) also satisfies \\(b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\nBecause \\(a\\le n\\), the number of admissible values of \\(a\\) is at most\n\\[\n   \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-a_0}{q}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\\;\\le\\;\n   \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{q}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n*If \\(q\\ge2\\)*, then \\(\\bigl\\lfloor n/q\\bigr\\rfloor+1\\le\n\\bigl\\lfloor n/2\\bigr\\rfloor+1\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).\n\n*If \\(q=1\\)*, the slope is an integer \\(p\\neq0,-1\\).  \nFrom the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain\n\\(a+pa+b_0\\le n+1\\), i.e. \\(a\\le\\frac{n+1-b_0}{p+1}\\) when \\(p>-1\\) and\n\\(a\\le\\frac{n+1-b_0}{1-p}\\) when \\(p<-1\\).  In either case the bound\n\\(a\\le\\frac{n+1}{2}\\) holds, so the number of integer points on \\(\\ell\\) does\nnot exceed \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).\n\nConsequently **every sunny line contains at most**\n\\[\n   M_n:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\npoints of \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Impossibility of \\(k=n\\) for \\(n\\ge4\\)\n\nAssume that all \\(n\\) lines are sunny.  Let \\(P\\) be the set of points of\n\\(T_n\\) that are covered by at least one of the lines.  Because a point may\nbelong to several lines, the number of *distinct* points covered is at most the\nsum of the maximal numbers of points that each line can contribute.  Using\n(7) we obtain\n\\[\n   |P|\\;\\le\\; n\\;M_n\n   \\;=\\; n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nFrom (1) we have \\(|T_n|=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}{2}\\).  For every integer \\(n\\ge4\\)\nthe strict inequality\n\\[\n   n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   \\;<\\; \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\nholds (it is an equality only when \\(n\\) is odd and \\(n=3\\)).  Hence\n\\(|P|<|T_n|\\); i.e. the \\(n\\) sunny lines cannot cover all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\nTherefore\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For every }n\\ge4\\text{ a configuration with }k=n\\text{ does not exist}.}\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Summary of the obtained information  \n\n*   For **every** \\(n\\ge3\\) the value \\(k=0\\) is realizable (Section\u202f2).  \n*   For **every** \\(n\\ge4\\) the value \\(k=n\\) is impossible (Section\u202f4).  \n\nThe status of the intermediate values \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\) (and the special case\n\\(k=n\\) when \\(n=3\\)) remains open within the present work.  The two rigorous\nstatements above constitute the maximal partial progress that could be\nestablished."
