agonal of slope \\(-1\\))\ncontains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe longest horizontal line inside the triangle is \\(y=1\\); it contains the\npoints \\((1,1),(2,1),\\dots,(n,1)\\), i.e. \\(n\\) points.  The same bound holds\nfor a vertical line \\(x=1\\) and for the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\).  Any other line\nof the same family is a sub‑segment of one of these three, therefore it also\ncontains at most \\(n\\) points. ∎\n\n\n\nLet exactly \\(k\\) of the \\(n\\) lines be sunny.  By Lemma 1 each sunny line\ncovers at most two points, and by Lemma 2 each non‑sunny line covers at most\n\\(n\\) points.  Consequently\n\n\\[\n|T_n|\\le 2k+n\\,(n-k)=n^{2}-k(n-2).\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(|T_n|=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}2\\) (formula (1)), inequality (6) yields\n\n\\[\nk\\le\\frac{n^{2}-\\frac{n(n+1)}2}{\\,n-2\\,}\n   =\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}.\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n\\ge5\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}=\n\\frac{n+1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2(n-2)}\\in\\Bigl(\\frac{n+1}{2},\\frac{n+1}{2}+1\\Bigr),\n\\]\n\nhence\n\n\\[\nk\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor.\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nFor the two small values \\(n=3,4\\) the right–hand side of (7) equals \\(3\\).\nThus any admissible configuration must satisfy\n\n\\[\nk\\le\n\\begin{cases}\n3, & n=3,4,\\\\[2mm]\n\\displaystyle\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor, & n\\ge5.\n\\end{cases}\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Constructions attaining every integer allowed by (9)\n\nWe now show that for each integer \\(k\\) satisfying (9) there exists a set of\n\\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines covering all points of\n\\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1  The general construction for \\(n\\ge5\\)\n\nAssume \\(k\\) satisfies (8).  Because \\(|T_n|= \\frac{n(n+1)}2\\ge 2k\\), we can\nchoose \\(k\\) disjoint unordered pairs of points of \\(T_n\\).  For each pair\n\\(\\{P_i,Q_i\\}\\) we draw th