            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe condition of the problem is that the \\(n\\) given lines together contain\nevery point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Counting bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope \\(\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n*Lemma 1.*  \nA sunny line meets \\(T_n\\) in at most two points.\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume a sunny line contains three distinct points\n\\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2),(a_3,b_3)\\in T_n\\) with \\(a_1<a_2<a_3\\).\nThen the slopes \\(\\frac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1}\\) and\n\\(\\frac{b_3-b_2}{a_3-a_2}\\) are equal.  Both numerators and denominators are\nintegers, so the common slope is a rational number with denominator\n\\(a_2-a_1\\) or \\(a_3-a_2\\).  Because all three points satisfy\n\\(a_i+b_i\\le n+1\\), the only possible rational slopes are \\(0,\\infty\\) or\n\\(-1\\); otherwise the three points would violate the inequality\n\\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  Hence a sunny line cannot contain three points of \\(T_n\\). ∎\n\n\n\n*Lemma 2.*  \nA non‑sunny line (horizontal, vertical or diagonal of slope \\(-1\\))\ncontains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe longest horizontal line inside the triangle is \\(y=1\\); it contains the\npoints \\((1,1),(2,1),\\dots,(n,1)\\), i.e. \\(n\\) points.  The same bound holds\nfor a vertical line \\(x=1\\) and for the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\).  Any other line\nof the same family is a sub‑segment of one of these three, therefore it also\ncontains at most \\(n\\) points. ∎\n\n\n\nLet exactly \\(k\\) of the \\(n\\) lines be sunny.  By Lemma 1 each sunny line\ncovers at most two points, and by Lemma 2 each non‑sunny line covers at most\n\\(n\\) points.  Consequently\n\n\\[\n|T_n|\\le 2k+n\\,(n-k)=n^{2}-k(n-2).\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(|T_n|=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}2\\) (formula (1)), inequality (6) yields\n\n\\[\nk\\le\\frac{n^{2}-\\frac{n(n+1)}2}{\\,n-2\\,}\n   =\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}.\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n\\ge5\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}=\n\\frac{n+1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2(n-2)}\\in\\Bigl(\\frac{n+1}{2},\\frac{n+1}{2}+1\\Bigr),\n\\]\n\nhence\n\n\\[\nk\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor.\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nFor the two small values \\(n=3,4\\) the right–hand side of (7) equals \\(3\\).\nThus any admissible configuration must satisfy\n\n\\[\nk\\le\n\\begin{cases}\n3, & n=3,4,\\\\[2mm]\n\\displaystyle\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor, & n\\ge5.\n\\end{cases}\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Constructions attaining every integer allowed by (9)\n\nWe now show that for each integer \\(k\\) satisfying (9) there exists a set of\n\\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines covering all points of\n\\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1  The general construction for \\(n\\ge5\\)\n\nAssume \\(k\\) satisfies (8).  Because \\(|T_n|= \\frac{n(n+1)}2\\ge 2k\\), we can\nchoose \\(k\\) disjoint unordered pairs of points of \\(T_n\\).  For each pair\n\\(\\{P_i,Q_i\\}\\) we draw the line \\(\\ell_i\\) through the two points; we\nselect the pairs so that the slope of \\(\\ell_i\\) is different from\n\\(0,\\infty,-1\\) (this is always possible because among the many possible\npairs at least one has a non‑forbidden slope).  The lines\n\\(\\ell_1,\\dots,\\ell_k\\) are distinct, sunny, and each covers exactly the two\nchosen points.\n\nRemove the \\(2k\\) points already covered.  The remaining set \\(R\\) has\n\n\\[\n|R|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2-2k\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nelements.  Consider the horizontal lines\n\n\\[\ny=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=n-k .\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nThere are exactly \\(n-k\\) of them, they are non‑sunny, and they are pairwise\ndistinct.  The line \\(y=r\\) contains precisely \\(n+1-r\\) points of \\(T_n\\);\nhence the total number of points covered by the family (11) equals\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{r=1}^{n-k}(n+1-r)=\\frac{(n-k)(n-k+1)}2.\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nBecause of inequality (6) with the present value of \\(k\\), we have\n\n\\[\n\\frac{(n-k)(n-k+1)}2\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}2-2k,\n\\]\n\ni.e. the right–hand side of (12) is at least \\(|R|\\).  Therefore the\nhorizontal lines (11) cover every point of \\(R\\).\n\nConsequently the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(\\ell_i\\) together with the\n\\(n-k\\) horizontals (11) form a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers\nall points of \\(T_n\\).  The construction works for every integer\n\\(k\\) satisfying (8).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  The exceptional cases \\(n=3,4\\)\n\n*Case \\(n=3\\).*  \n\\(T_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1),(1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\\).\n\n* \\(k=3\\): three sunny lines  \n\n  \\[\n  \\begin{aligned}\n  \\ell_1 &: y=x \\quad\\text{through }(1,1),(2,2),\\\\\n  \\ell_2 &: y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac32 \\quad\\text{through }(1,2),(3,1),\\\\\n  \\ell_3 &: y=-2x+4 \\quad\\text{through }(1,3),(2,1).\n  \\end{aligned}\n  \\]\n\n  They are pairwise distinct, none has slope \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), and together\n  they contain all six points.\n\n* \\(k=2\\): keep \\(\\ell_1,\\ell_2\\) above and add the horizontal line\n  \\(y=1\\); this covers the remaining point \\((2,1)\\).\n\n* \\(k=1\\): keep \\(\\ell_1\\) and add the horizontals \\(y=1\\) and \\(y=2\\).\n\n* \\(k=0\\): use the three horizontals \\(y=1,2,3\\).\n\nThus every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\) is realizable.\n\n*Case \\(n=4\\).*  \n\\(T_4\\) consists of ten points.  The following families work.\n\n* \\(k=3\\):  \n\n  \\[\n  \\begin{aligned}\n  \\ell_1 &: y=x \\quad\\text{through }(1,1),(2,2),\\\\\n  \\ell_2 &: y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52 \\quad\\text{through }(1,4),(3,3),\\\\\n  \\ell_3 &: y=-2x+6 \\quad\\text{through }(2,4),(3,2).\n  \\end{aligned}\n  \\]\n\n  The remaining points are all on the horizontal line \\(y=1\\).\n\n* \\(k=2\\): keep \\(\\ell_1,\\ell_2\\) and add the horizontals \\(y=1\\) and\n  \\(y=2\\).\n\n* \\(k=1\\): keep \\(\\ell_1\\) and add the horizontals \\(y=1,2,3\\).\n\n* \\(k=0\\): use the four horizontals \\(y=1,2,3,4\\).\n\nHence every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\) is attainable for \\(n=4\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Final answer\n\nCombining the impossibility bound (9) with the explicit constructions of\nSections 2.1 and 2.2, we obtain the complete description of the admissible\nvalues of \\(k\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{array}{ll}\n\\text{If }n=3\\text{ or }n=4:& k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\};\\\\[2mm]\n\\text{If }n\\ge5:& k\\in\\Bigl\\{0,1,\\dots,\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr\\}.\n\\end{array}}\n\\]\n\nThese are exactly the non‑negative integers for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct\nlines satisfying the two conditions of the problem.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
