            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A bound on the number of points a sunny line can contain  \n\nFor a fixed slope \\(m\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\) the equation of a line is \\(y=mx+c\\) (or \\(x=c\\) if \\(m=\\infty\\), which is excluded).  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) lies on the line, then  \n\n\\[\nb=ma+c,\\qquad a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1 .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are integers, the set of admissible \\(a\\)’s is an arithmetic progression with difference \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{1}{\\gcd(p,q)}\\) where \\(m=\\frac{p}{q}\\) in lowest terms.  \nConsequently the number of integer points of \\(T_n\\) on the line is at most the length of the longest segment of the line that stays inside the triangle, i.e. at most the number of integer points on a line of slope \\(1\\) inside the same triangle.  \nA line of slope \\(1\\) meets the triangle in the points  \n\n\\[\n(1,1+d),(2,2+d),\\dots ,\\bigl(\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rfloor,\\;\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rfloor+d\\bigr),\n\\]\n\nhence at most  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,M_n:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\,}\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(T_n\\).  Therefore every sunny line contains at most \\(M_n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Impossibility of \\(k=n\\) when \\(n\\) is even  \n\nAssume that all \\(n\\) lines are sunny.  \nEach of them can cover at most \\(M_n\\) points, so the total number of points that can be covered is at most  \n\n\\[\nnM_n=n\\cdot\\frac{n}{2}= \\frac{n^{2}}2 .\n\\]\n\nBecause  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2=\\frac{n^{2}}2+\\frac{n}{2}> \\frac{n^{2}}2 ,\n\\]\n\nthe whole set \\(T_n\\) cannot be covered.  Hence for even \\(n\\) a configuration with \\(k=n\\) sunny lines does **not** exist.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  A configuration with *all* lines sunny when \\(n\\) is odd  \n\nWrite \\(n=2t+1\\) (\\(t\\ge1\\)).  \nFor each integer \\(d\\) with \\(-t\\le d\\le t\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_d:\\; y=x+d .\n\\]\n\nAll the lines \\(L_d\\) have slope \\(1\\) (sunny) and are distinct because their intercepts differ.  \n\nFor a fixed \\(d\\) the points of \\(T_n\\) satisfying \\(b-a=d\\) are  \n\n\\[\n(1,1+d),(2,2+d),\\dots ,(t+1,t+1+d),\n\\]\n\nexactly \\(t+1=\\dfrac{n+1}{2}=M_n\\) points.  \nSince the differences \\(b-a\\) of points of \\(T_n\\) range precisely over the integers \\(-t,-t+1,\\dots ,t\\), every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on exactly one of the lines \\(L_d\\).  \n\nThus the family \\(\\{L_d\\mid -t\\le d\\le t\\}\\) consists of \\(2t+1=n\\) distinct sunny lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\).  Consequently for odd \\(n\\) the value \\(k=n\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  A configuration with \\(k=n-1\\) sunny lines when \\(n\\) is even  \n\nLet \\(n=2t\\).  \nTake the horizontal line  \n\n\\[\nH:\\; y=1 .\n\\]\n\nIt contains the \\(n\\) points \\((1,1),(2,1),\\dots ,(n,1)\\).  \nDelete these points from \\(T_n\\); the remaining set is  \n\n\\[\nT_{n-1}'=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge2,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nPutting \\(b'=b-1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nT_{n-1}'=\\{(a,b')\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b'\\ge1,\\;a+b'\\le n\\}=T_{n-1}.\n\\]\n\nNow \\(n-1=2t-1\\) is odd, so by the construction of Section 3 the triangle \\(T_{n-1}\\) can be covered by exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines of slope \\(1\\).  \nAdding the horizontal line \\(H\\) we obtain a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines, precisely \\(n-1\\) of which are sunny.  Hence for even \\(n\\) the value \\(k=n-1\\) is realizable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  From the maximal number of sunny lines to any smaller number  \n\nAssume we already have a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) and containing \\(k_{\\max}\\) sunny lines (the families constructed in Sections 3 and 4).  \n\nPick any sunny line \\(L\\in\\mathcal F\\); write its equation as \\(y=x+d\\) (the only sunny lines we ever use have slope \\(1\\)).  \nThe *lowest* point of \\(L\\) inside \\(T_n\\) is \\((1,1+d)\\).  \nReplace \\(L\\) by the horizontal line  \n\n\\[\nH_{d}:\\; y=1+d .\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(H_{d}\\) is non‑sunny (parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis) and certainly contains the point \\((1,1+d)\\); all other points of \\(L\\) are still covered because they belong to other lines of \\(\\mathcal F\\) (the construction of Sections 3 and 4 guarantees that each point of \\(T_n\\) lies on exactly one line of slope \\(1\\)).  Consequently the replacement does **not** lose any point of \\(T_n\\).\n\nPerforming this replacement on any subset of the sunny lines we obtain, for every integer  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le k_{\\max},\n\\]\n\na family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers \\(T_n\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are the horizontal lines introduced by the replacements).  \n\nThus every integer between \\(0\\) and the maximal value found in Sections 3–4 is attainable.\n\n-------------------------------------