            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Maximum number of lattice points of a sunny line in \\(T_n\\))\n\nLet \\(L\\) be a sunny line. Write its reduced rational slope as \\(\\frac{p}{q}\\) with \\(q>0\\) and \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\).  \nIf \\((a_0,b_0)\\in L\\cap\\mathbb Z^2\\), then every other integer point of \\(L\\) has the form \\((a_0+mq,\\;b_0+mp)\\) with \\(m\\in\\mathbb Z\\).  \nHence the difference in the \\(a\\)-coordinate between two consecutive lattice points on \\(L\\) is at least \\(q\\ge1\\).  \n\nInside the triangle \\(T_n\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\). Consequently the number of integer points of \\(L\\) that can lie in \\(T_n\\) is at most  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{q}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\n(The worst case is \\(q=1\\), i.e. slope an integer; then the bound becomes \\(\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rfloor\\).)  \nThus\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,|L\\cap T_n|\\le M_n:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\,}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Impossibility of \\(k=n\\) for even \\(n\\))\n\nAssume that all \\(n\\) lines are sunny. By Lemma 1 each of them contains at most \\(M_n\\) points of \\(T_n\\); therefore\n\n\\[\n|T_n|\\le nM_n=n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n      =n\\cdot\\frac{n}{2}= \\frac{n^{2}}2 .\n\\]\n\nBut \\(|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2>\\frac{n^{2}}2\\) for every even \\(n\\ge4\\).  \nHence a configuration with \\(k=n\\) sunny lines cannot exist when \\(n\\) is even. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Construction for any \\(k\\le n-1\\))\n\nConsider the family of horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\nH_j:\\;y=j\\qquad(j=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nEvery point of \\(T_n\\) lies on exactly one of them, so the family \\(\\{H_j\\}_{j=1}^{n}\\) is a feasible configuration with \\(k=0\\).\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\). Choose an integer \\(s\\) with  \n\n\\[\ns\\neq0,\\;s\\neq-1,\\qquad |s|>n .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nDefine the line  \n\n\\[\nL_{a,b,s}:\\; y-sx = b-sa .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(|s|>n\\), the line (3) meets the triangle \\(T_n\\) only at the point \\((a,b)\\); any other integer point \\((a',b')\\) on the line would satisfy  \n\n\\[\nb'-b = s(a'-a),\n\\]\n\nand the left‑hand side has absolute value at most \\(n\\) (since both coordinates lie between \\(1\\) and \\(n\\)), whereas the right‑hand side has absolute value at least \\(|s|\\ge n+1\\) unless \\(a'=a\\). Hence no other lattice point of \\(T_n\\) lies on \\(L_{a,b,s}\\).\n\nNow replace the horizontal line \\(H_b\\) (which contains \\((a,b)\\)) by the sunny line \\(L_{a,b,s}\\).  \nAll points of \\(T_n\\) remain covered, and the number of sunny lines increases by one.\n\nRepeating this replacement for any collection of distinct points of \\(T_n\\) yields, for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\), a configuration consisting of\n\n* \\(k\\) sunny lines, each covering a single point of \\(T_n\\);\n* the remaining \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines, which together cover the other points.\n\nThus every \\(k\\) in the range \\(0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\) is attainable. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4 (Existence of \\(k=n\\) for \\(n=3\\))\n\nFor \\(n=3\\) we have  \n\n\\[\nT_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe three sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_1:\\; y=2x-1,\\qquad\n\\ell_2:\\; y=3x-2,\\qquad\n\\ell_3:\\; y=5x-4\n\\]\n\nhave slopes \\(2,3,5\\) respectively (none of which is \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)) and satisfy  \n\n\\[\n(1,1)\\in\\ell_1,\\qquad (1,2)\\in\\ell_2,\\qquad (2,1)\\in\\ell_3 .\n\\]\n\nHence all three points are covered and all three lines are sunny; therefore \\(k=3\\) is possible when \\(n=3\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Partial Result for odd \\(n\\ge5\\)\n\nWhen \\(n\\) is odd, Lemma 1 gives \\(M_n=\\frac{n+1}{2}\\) and consequently  \n\n\\[\nnM_n = n\\cdot\\frac{n+1}{2}=|T_n|.\n\\]\n\nThus the counting argument does **not** rule out a configuration with \\(k=n\\).  \nI have not been able to either construct such a configuration for any odd \\(n\\ge5\\) or to prove that it cannot exist. Consequently the status of the value \\(k=n\\) for odd \\(n\\ge5\\) remains open in the present work.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\n* For every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the values  \n\n  \\[\n  k=0,1,\\dots ,n-1\n  \\]\n\n  are realizable (Lemma 3).\n\n* The value \\(k=n\\) is realizable **only** for \\(n=3\\) (Lemma 4).\n\n* For even \\(n\\ge4\\) the value \\(k=n\\) is impossible (Lemma 2).\n\n* For odd \\(n\\ge5\\) the possibility of \\(k=n\\) is not settled by the arguments presented here.\n\nHence the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\ \\text{for all }n\\ge3,\\quad\\text{and additionally }k=n\\text{ when }n=3.}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, genera