-------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Construction for any \\(k\\le n-1\\))\n\nConsider the family of horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\nH_j:\\;y=j\\qquad(j=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nEvery point of \\(T_n\\) lies on exactly one of them, so the family \\(\\{H_j\\}_{j=1}^{n}\\) is a feasible configuration with \\(k=0\\).\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\). Choose an integer \\(s\\) with  \n\n\\[\ns\\neq0,\\;s\\neq-1,\\qquad |s|>n .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nDefine the line  \n\n\\[\nL_{a,b,s}:\\; y-sx = b-sa .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(|s|>n\\), the line (3) meets the triangle \\(T_n\\) only at the point \\((a,b)\\); any other integer point \\((a',b')\\) on the line would satisfy  \n\n\\[\nb'-b = s(a'-a),\n\\]\n\nand the left‑hand side has absolute value at most \\(n\\) (since both coordinates lie between \\(1\\) and \\(n\\)), whereas the right‑hand side has absolute value at least \\(|s|\\ge n+1\\) unless \\(a'=a\\). Hence no other lattice point of \\(T_n\\) lies on \\(L_{a,b,s}\\).\n\nNow replace the horizontal line \\(H_b\\) (which contains \\((a,b)\\)) by the sunny line \\(L_{a,b,s}\\).  \nAll points of \\(T_n\\) remain covered, and the number of sunny lines increases by one.\n\nRepeating this replacement for any collection of distinct points of \\(T_n\\) yields, for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\), a configuration consisting of\n\n* \\(k\\) sunny lines, each covering a single point of \\(T_n\\);\n* the remaining \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines, which together cover the other points.\n\nThus every \\(k\\) in the range \\(0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\) is attainable. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4 (Existence of \\(k=n\\) for \\(n=3\\))\n\nFor \\(n=3\\) we have  \n\n\\[\nT_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe three sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_1:\\; y=2x-1,\\qquad\n\\ell_2:\\; y=3x-2,\\qquad\n\\ell_3:\\; y=5x-4\n\\]\n\nhave slopes \\(2,3,5\\) respectively (none of which is \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)) and s