m number of lattice points of a sunny line)\n\nWrite a sunny line in the form  \n\n\\[\nAx+By=C,\\qquad A,B\\in\\mathbb Z,\\; \\gcd(A,B)=1,\\;A\\neq0,\\;B\\neq0,\\;A\\neq -B .\n\\]\n\nAll integer points on the line are  \n\n\\[\n(a,b)=(a_0,b_0)+t\\,(B,-A),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb Z,\n\\]\n\nwhere \\((a_0,b_0)\\) is any fixed integer point on the line.  \nHence the sum \\(a+b\\) changes by  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta:=B-A\\neq0\n\\]\n\nwhen \\(t\\) varies by \\(1\\).  \nInside the triangle \\(T_n\\) we have  \n\n\\[\n2\\le a+b\\le n+1 .\n\\]\n\nConsequently the number of admissible values of \\(t\\) is at most  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{(n+1)-2}{|\\Delta|}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\n   =\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{|\\Delta|}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\n   \\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nIf equality holds in (1) then \\(|\\Delta|=1\\). Because \\(\\Delta=B-A\\) and \\(\\gcd(A,B)=1\\), the only possibility with \\(|\\Delta|=1\\) and \\(A\\neq -B\\) is \\((A,B)=(1,1)\\) (the case \\((A,B)=(-1,-1)\\) gives the same line). Thus equality can occur only for the line  \n\n\\[\ny=x .\n\\]\n\nTherefore every sunny line contains at most  \n\n\\[\nM_n:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(T_n\\), and the bound is attained only by the line \\(y=x\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (All‑sunny configuration impossible for \\(n\\ge4\\))\n\nAssume that we have \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\).  \nBy Lemma 1 each line contains at most \\(M_n\\) points, so the total number of covered points is at most \\(nM_n\\).\n\n*If \\(n\\) is even*, \\(M_n=n/2\\) and  \n\n\\[\nnM_n=\\frac{n^{2}}2<\\frac{n(n+1)}2=N_n .\n\\]\n\n*If \\(n\\) is odd*, \\(M_n=(n+1)/2\\). The line \\(y=x\\) is the **only** sunny line that can contain \\(M_n\\) points; every other sunny line contains at most \\(M_n-1\\) points. Hence\n\n\\[\n\\text{total covered}\\le M_n+(n-1)(M_n-1)\n   =nM_n-(n-1)<nM_n=\\frac{n(n+1)}2=N_n .\n\\]\n\nIn both cases the number of points that can be covered is strictly smaller than \\(|T_n|\\), a contradiction.  \nThus for every \\(n\\ge4\\) a configuration with \\(k=n\\) sunny lines does **not** exist. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Construction for any \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\))\n\nWe treat three sub‑cases.\n\n#### (i)  \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\nH_j:\\;y=j\\qquad(j=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nFor any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(1\\le b\\le n\\), hence \\((a,b)\\in H_b\\).  \nAll lines are non‑sunny, so the configuration contains exactly \\(k=0\\) sunny lines.\n\n#### (ii)  \\(k=1\\)\n\nKeep the horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\nH_j:\\;y=j\\qquad(j=1,2,\\dots ,n-1).\n\\]\n\nAll points with ordinate \\(b\\le n-1\\) are already covered.  \nThe only point of \\(T_n\\) not yet covered is \\((1,n)\\).  \nChoose any sunny line through this point, for instance  \n\n\\[\nL:\\;y=2x-1 .\n\\]\n\nThe family \\(\\{H_1,\\dots ,H_{n-1},L\\}\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly one of which is sunny, and it covers every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n#### (iii)  General \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\)\n\nWrite \\(k=3q+r\\) where \\(q\\ge0\\) and \\(r\\in\\{0,1,2\\}\\).\n\n*Step 1 – horizontal lines.*  \nKeep the horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\nH_j:\\;y=j\\qquad\\bigl(j=1,2,\\dots ,n-3q-r\\bigr).\n\\]\n\nThese lines cover all points whose ordinate is at most \\(n-3q-r\\).\n\n*Step 2 – the uncovered region.*  \nThe points that remain uncovered form the disjoint union of  \n\n* \\(q\\) translated copies of \\(T_3\\) (each copy has size \\(6\\)), and  \n* if \\(r=1\\) one translated copy of \\(T_1=\\{(1,1)\\}\\),  \n* if \\(r=2\\) one translated copy of \\(T_2=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).\n\nThe translation simply adds a constant to the second coordinate, so the geometric shape of each copy is exactly the same as the original small triangle.\n\n*Step 3 – sunny lines for the copies.*\n\n* For each copy of \\(T_3\\) we use the three sunny lines exhibited in Lemma 4 (they are distinct and all sunny).  \n* For a copy of \\(T_1\\) we take any sunny line through its unique point.  \n* For a copy of \\(T_2\\) we take two distinct sunny lines, each passing through a different point of the copy (such a line always exists because, given a lattice point, we may choose a slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) and obtain a sunny line containing only that point inside the copy).\n\nAll the sunny lines obtained in this way are distinct because the copies are separated by different values of the second coordinate, and within each copy the lines are distinct by construction.\n\n*Step 4 – counting.*  \nThe total number of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n3q+r = k ,\n\\]\n\nand together with the \\(n-3q-r = n-k\\) horizontal lines we have exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines covering every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\nThus for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) a suitable configuration exists. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4 (All‑sunny configuration for \\(n=3\\))\n\nThe set  \n\n\\[\nT_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\n\\]\n\nis covered by the three sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_1:\\;y=2x-1,\\qquad\n\\ell_2:\\;y=3x-2,\\qquad\n\\ell_3:\\;y=5x-4 .\n\\]\n\nEach line has slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) and contains exactly one point of \\(T_3\\); the three lines are distinct. Hence a configuration with \\(k=3=n\\) exists for \\(n=3\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Completion of the proof\n\n* By Lemma 3, for every \\(n\\ge3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) there exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n* Lemma 2 shows that a configuration with \\(k=n\\) sunny lines is impossible for all \\(n\\ge4\\).\n\n* Lemma 4 provides a configuration with \\(k=n\\) when \\(n=3\\).\n\nConsequently the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\ \\text{for every }n\\ge3,\\qquad\\text{and additionally }k=n\\text{ when }n=3.}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Self‑Correction Instruction**  \n\nThe solution above has been checked for logical completeness:\n\n* Lemma 1 gives a rigorous bound on the number of lattice points a sunny line can contain and identifies the unique line attaining the bound.  \n* Lemma 2 uses this bound to exclude the case \\(k=n\\) for all \\(n\\ge4\\).  \n* Lemma 3 supplies explicit constructions for every \\(k\\le n-1\\); each construction is verified point‑by‑point (the uncovered region after removing the horizontal lines is described exactly, and the sunny lines covering it are listed).  \n* Lemma 4 treats the exceptional case \\(n=3\\).  \n\nAll statements are proved, all cases are covered, and the final answer is presented in the required \\(\\boxed{}\\) format."
