\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\) (second coordinate \\(n-3q\\!+\\!1\\) and \\(n-3q\\!+\\!2\\)).\n\n*Sunny lines for each translate.*  \n\n* For each translate of \\(T_3\\) we use the three sunny lines from Lemma 4, shifted vertically by the same constant; they remain sunny because a vertical translation does not change the slope.\n* For a translate of \\(T_1\\) we take any sunny line through its single point (e.g. \\(y=2x-1\\) shifted appropriately).\n* For a translate of \\(T_2\\) we take two sunny lines, each passing through a different point of the translate; such lines exist because for any lattice point we can choose a slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) and obtain a sunny line containing only that point inside the translate.\n\nAll these sunny lines are distinct: translates are separated by different second‑coordinate offsets, and within each translate the lines are distinct by construction.\n\n*Counting.*  \nThe construction uses exactly \\(3q+r=k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-3q-r=n-k\\) horizontal lines, altogether \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\n*Verification of coverage.*  \nEvery point of \\(T_n\\) either lies on one of the kept horizontal lines (by definition of the horizontal strip) or belongs to one of the translates described above; in the latter case it lies on one of the sunny lines assigned to that translate. Hence every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered.\n\nThus for any \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) a required configuration exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4  \n\nThe three sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_1:\\;y=2x-1,\\qquad\n\\ell_2:\\;y=3x-2,\\qquad\n\\ell_3:\\;y=5x-4\n\\]\n\ncontain respectively the points \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\) of \\(T_3\\).  \nTheir slopes \\(2,3,5\\) are different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); the lines are distinct and all sunny. Hence \\(T_3\\) can be covered by three sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Completion of the proof\n\n* 