            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad N_n:=|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor to the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \n\nWrite a sunny line as  \n\n\\[\nAx+By=C,\\qquad A,B\\in\\mathbb Z,\\; \\gcd(A,B)=1,\\;A\\neq0,\\;B\\neq0,\\;A\\neq -B .\n\\]\n\nAll integer points on the line are  \n\n\\[\n(a,b)=(a_0,b_0)+t\\,(B,-A),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb Z,\n\\]\n\nwhere \\((a_0,b_0)\\) is any fixed integer point on the line.  \nHence the sum \\(a+b\\) changes by  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta:=B-A\\neq0\n\\]\n\nwhen \\(t\\) increases by \\(1\\).  \nInside \\(T_n\\) we have \\(2\\le a+b\\le n+1\\); therefore the number of admissible values of \\(t\\) is at most  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{(n+1)-2}{|\\Delta|}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\n   =\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{|\\Delta|}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\n   \\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nIf equality holds in (1) then \\(|\\Delta|=1\\). Because \\(\\gcd(A,B)=1\\) and \\(A\\neq -B\\), the only possibility is \\((A,B)=(1,1)\\) (or \\((-1,-1)\\)), which gives the line \\(y=x\\). Consequently\n\n* every sunny line meets at most \\(M_n:=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points of \\(T_n\\);\n* only the line \\(y=x\\) can meet exactly \\(M_n\\) points; all other sunny lines meet at most \\(M_n-1\\) points.\n\n∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2  \n\nAssume that all \\(n\\) lines of a required configuration are sunny.  \nBy Lemma 1 at most one of them can contain \\(M_n\\) points of \\(T_n\\); the remaining \\(n-1\\) lines contain at most \\(M_n-1\\) points each.  \nHence the total number of **distinct** points covered is at most  \n\n\\[\nM_n+(n-1)(M_n-1)=nM_n-(n-1).\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n\\ge4\\) we have \\(nM_n-(n-1)<\\frac{n(n+1)}2=N_n\\):\n*If \\(n\\) is even*, \\(M_n=n/2\\) and \\(nM_n-(n-1)=\\frac{n^{2}}2-(n-1)<\\frac{n^{2}+n}{2}=N_n\\).  \n*If \\(n\\) is odd*, \\(M_n=(n+1)/2\\) and  \n\n\\[\nnM_n-(n-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2-(n-1)<\\frac{n(n+1)}2=N_n .\n\\]\n\nThus the \\(n\\) sunny lines cannot cover all \\(N_n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  \nTherefore a configuration with \\(k=n\\) sunny lines does **not** exist for any \\(n\\ge4\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3  \n\nWe construct, for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\), a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### (i)  \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\nH_j:\\;y=j\\qquad(j=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nFor any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(1\\le b\\le n\\), hence \\((a,b)\\in H_b\\).  \nAll lines are parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, so none is sunny.  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### (ii)  \\(k=1\\)\n\nKeep the horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\nH_j:\\;y=j\\qquad(j=1,2,\\dots ,n-1).\n\\]\n\nAll points with \\(b\\le n-1\\) are covered.  \nThe only uncovered point is \\((1,n)\\).  \nChoose a sunny line through it, e.g.  \n\n\\[\nL:\\;y=2x-1 .\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(\\{H_1,\\dots ,H_{n-1},L\\}\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly one of which is sunny, and every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on one of them.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### (iii)  \\(k=2\\) when \\(n=3\\)\n\nThe horizontal line \\(H_1:\\;y=1\\) covers the points \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,1)\\).  \nThe remaining points are \\((1,2),(1,3)\\).  \nThe sunny line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_1:\\;y=-2x+5\n\\]\n\npasses through \\((2,1)\\) and \\((1,3)\\); the sunny line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_2:\\;y=2x\n\\]\n\npasses through \\((1,2)\\).  \nThus \\(\\{H_1,\\ell_1,\\ell_2\\}\\) is a configuration with exactly two sunny lines covering all of \\(T_3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### (iv)  General \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\)\n\nWrite \\(k=3q+r\\) where \\(q\\ge0\\) and \\(r\\in\\{0,1,2\\}\\).\n\n*Horizontal strip.*  \nKeep the horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\nH_j:\\;y=j\\qquad\\bigl(j=1,2,\\dots ,n-3q-r\\bigr).\n\\]\n\nThese lines cover every point of \\(T_n\\) whose second coordinate is at most \\(n-3q-r\\).\n\n*Uncovered region.*  \nThe points not covered by the horizontal strip form a disjoint union of:\n* \\(q\\) translates of  \n\n  \\[\n  T_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\},\n  \\]\n  obtained by adding the same constant \\(c\\) to the second coordinate, where the constants are\n  \\[\n  c_i=n-3q-r+1+3(i-1)\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,q).\n  \\]\n* If \\(r=1\\) one translate of \\(T_1=\\{(1,1)\\}\\) (second coordinate \\(n-3q\\!+\\!1\\)).\n* If \\(r=2\\) one translate of \\(T_2=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\) (second coordinate \\(n-3q\\!+\\!1\\) and \\(n-3q\\!+\\!2\\)).\n\n*Sunny lines for each translate.*  \n\n* For each translate of \\(T_3\\) we use the three sunny lines from Lemma 4, shifted vertically by the same constant; they remain sunny because a vertical translation does not change the slope.\n* For a translate of \\(T_1\\) we take any sunny line through its single point (e.g. \\(y=2x-1\\) shifted appropriately).\n* For a translate of \\(T_2\\) we take two sunny lines, each passing through a different point of the translate; such lines exist because for any lattice point we can choose a slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) and obtain a sunny line containing only that point inside the translate.\n\nAll these sunny lines are distinct: translates are separated by different second‑coordinate offsets, and within each translate the lines are distinct by construction.\n\n*Counting.*  \nThe construction uses exactly \\(3q+r=k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-3q-r=n-k\\) horizontal lines, altogether \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\n*Verification of coverage.*  \nEvery point of \\(T_n\\) either lies on one of the kept horizontal lines (by definition of the horizontal strip) or belongs to one of the translates described above; in the latter case it lies on one of the sunny lines assigned to that translate. Hence every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered.\n\nThus for any \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) a required configuration exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4  \n\nThe three sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_1:\\;y=2x-1,\\qquad\n\\ell_2:\\;y=3x-2,\\qquad\n\\ell_3:\\;y=5x-4\n\\]\n\ncontain respectively the points \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\) of \\(T_3\\).  \nTheir slopes \\(2,3,5\\) are different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); the lines are distinct and all sunny. Hence \\(T_3\\) can be covered by three sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Completion of the proof\n\n* Lemma 3 shows that for every \\(n\\ge3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) a configuration exists.\n* Lemma 2 proves that a configuration with \\(k=n\\) is impossible for all \\(n\\ge4\\).\n* Lemma 4 (together with the explicit construction for \\(k=2\\) when \\(n=3\\)) shows that \\(k=n\\) **is** possible when \\(n=3\\).\n\nConsequently the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\ \\text{for every }n\\ge3,\\qquad\\text{and additionally }k=n\\text{ when }n=3.}\n\\]\n\n∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
