ith \\(A=-B\\) (slope 1) are perfectly sunny, so the condition excludes admissible lines.\n\n> “All integer points on the line are  \n> \\[\n> (a,b)=(a_0,b_0)+t\\,(B,-A),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb Z,\n> \\]”\n\n*Correctness:* This is a standard description of the integer lattice points on a line with primitive normal vector \\((A,B)\\). No problem.\n\n> “Hence the sum \\(a+b\\) changes by  \n> \\[\n> \\Delta:=B-A\\neq0\n> \\] when \\(t\\) increases by \\(1\\).”\n\n**Issue 2 – Unjustified claim \\(\\Delta\\neq0\\) (Critical Error).**  \nThe solution deduces \\(\\Delta\\neq0\\) from the (incorrect) condition \\(A\\neq -B\\). In fact \\(\\Delta=0\\) occurs precisely when \\(A=B\\), i.e. for lines parallel to \\(x+y=0\\), which are *not* sunny. The correct sunny‑line condition is \\(A\\neq B\\); the proof should have used this. Because the argument relies on a false premise, the conclusion that \\(\\Delta\\neq0\\) for all sunny lines is not rigorously established.\n\n> “Inside \\(T_n\\) we have \\(2\\le a+b\\le n+1\\); therefore the number of admissible values of \\(t\\) is at most … \\(\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).”\n\n*Correctness of the bound (provided \\(\\Delta\\neq0\\))*: If \\(|\\Delta|\\ge1\\), the number of integer values of \\(t\\) that keep \\(a+b\\) within the interval \\([2,n+1]\\) is indeed at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{|\\Delta|}\\bigr\\rfloor+1\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\). The algebraic manipulation is fine, but it depends on the previously unproved \\(\\Delta\\neq0\\).\n\n> “If equality holds in (1) then \\(|\\Delta|=1\\). Because \\(\\gcd(A,B)=1\\) and \\(A\\neq -B\\), the only possibility is \\((A,B)=(1,1)\\) (or \\((-1,-1)\\)), which gives the line \\(y=x\\).”\n\n**Issue 3 – Misidentification of the line achieving equality (Critical Error).**  \nFor \\(|\\Delta|=|B-A|=1\\) the primitive solutions are \\((A,B)=(1,2)\\), \\((2,1)\\), \\((0,1)\\) etc., not uniquely \\((1,1)\\). Moreover, \\((A,B)=(1,1)\\) yields the line \\(x+y=C\\), which is *parallel* to \\(x+y=0\\) and therefore **not sunny**. The line \\(y=x\\) corresponds to \\((A,B)=(1,-1)\\), giving \\(\\Delta=-2\\). Hence the claim that the only sunny line attaining the maximal number of points is \\(y=x\\) is false.\n\n> “Consequently … every sunny line meets at most \\(M_n:=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points of \\(T_n\\); only the line \\(y=x\\) can meet exactly \\(M_n\\) points; all other sunny lines meet at most \\(M_n-1\\) points.”\n\n**Issue 4 – Result of Lemma 1 not proved (Critical Error).**  \nBecause the preceding arguments are flawed, the stated bound and the uniqueness of the extremal line are not established. The lemma’s conclusion is therefore unsupported.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Lemma 2\n\n> “Assume that all \\(n\\) lines of a required configuration are sunny.  \n> By Lemma 1 at most one of them can contain \\(M_n\\) points … the remaining \\(n-1\\) lines contain at most \\(M_n-1\\) points each.  \n> Hence the total number of distinct points covered is at most  \n> \\[\n> M_n+(n-1)(M_n-1)=nM_n-(n-1).\n> \\]”\n\n*Dependence on Lemma 1:* The bound relies entirely on the (incorrect) statement that a sunny line can cover at most \\(M_n\\) points and that only one line can achieve this. Since Lemma 1 is invalid, the bound in Lemma 2 is not justified.\n\n> “For \\(n\\ge4\\) we have \\(nM_n-(n-1)<\\frac{n(n+1)}2=N_n\\)… Thus the \\(n\\) sunny lines cannot cover all \\(N_n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).”\n\n**Issue 5 – Unproved impossibility (Critical Error).**  \nThe inequality \\(nM_n-(n-1)<N_n\\) is arithmetically correct, but because the preceding bound on coverage is unproved, the conclusion that a configuration with \\(k=n\\) sunny lines is impossible for \\(n\\ge4\\) is not established.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Lemma 3 – Constructions for \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\)\n\n#### (i) \\(k=0\\)\n\n> “Take the horizontal lines \\(H_j: y=j\\) for \\(j=1,\\dots,n\\). … All lines are parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, so none is sunny.”\n\n*Correctness:* This construction indeed covers every point of \\(T_n\\) (since \\(1\\le b\\le n\\)) and uses no sunny lines. No issue.\n\n#### (ii) \\(k=1\\)\n\n> “Keep the horizontal lines \\(H_j: y=j\\) for \\(j=1,\\dots,n-1\\). … The only uncovered point is \\((1,n)\\). Choose a sunny line through it, e.g. \\(L: y=2x-1\\).”\n\n*Correctness:* The horizontal strip covers all points with \\(b\\le n-1\\). The point \\((1,n)\\) belongs to \\(T_n\\) and is covered by the chosen line, which is not parallel to any forbidden direction. Thus a configuration with exactly one sunny line exists. No issue.\n\n#### (iii) \\(k=2\\) when \\(n=3\\)\n\n> “The horizontal line \\(H_1: y=1\\) covers the points \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,1)\\).  \n> The remaining points are \\((1,2),(1,3)\\).  \n> The sunny line \\(\\ell_1: y=-2x+5\\) passes through \\((2,1)\\) and \\((1,3)\\); the sunny line \\(\\ell_2: y=2x\\) passes through \\((1,2)\\).  \n> Thus \\(\\{H_1,\\ell_1,\\ell_2\\}\\) is a configuration with exactly two sunny lines covering all of \\(T_3\\).”\n\n**Issue 6 – Construction does not cover all points (Critical Error).**  \nThe set \\(T_3\\) (by the problem’s definition) consists of six points:\n\\[\n\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}.\n\\]\nThe three lines listed cover \\((1,1),(2,1),(1,3),(1,2)\\) but **miss** \\((2,2)\\) and \\((3,1)\\). Hence the claimed configuration fails to satisfy the covering requirement.\n\n#### (iv) General \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\)\n\n> “Write \\(k=3q+r\\) where \\(q\\ge0\\) and \\(r\\in\\{0,1,2\\}\\).  \n> … Keep the horizontal lines \\(H_j: y=j\\) for \\(j=1,\\dots,n-3q-r\\).  \n> … The points not covered … form a disjoint union of:  \n> * \\(q\\) translates of \\(T_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\), …  \n> * If \\(r=1\\) one translate of \\(T_1=\\{(1,1)\\}\\), …  \n> * If \\(r=2\\) one translate of \\(T_2=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\), …”\n\n**Issue 7 – Incorrect description of the sets \\(T_3,T_2,T_1\\) (Critical Error).**  \nAccording to the problem’s definition, \\(T_m\\) contains \\(\\frac{m(m+1)}2\\) points, not the three‑point set claimed for \\(T_3\\). The solution mistakenly uses the set of points with \\(a+b\\le m\\) (off by one) and consequently builds a construction that does **not** cover the true points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n> “For each translate of \\(T_3\\) we use the three sunny lines from Lemma 4, shifted vertically …”\n\n**Issue 8 – Lemma 4 does not cover the true \\(T_3\\) (Critical Error).**  \nLemma 4 states that three sunny lines \\(\\ell_1,\\ell_2,\\ell_3\\) contain respectively the points \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\) of \\(T_3\\). Since the actual \\(T_3\\) has six points, these three lines leave half the points uncovered. Therefore the claimed “covering” is false.\n\n> “All these sunny lines are distinct … The construction uses exactly \\(3q+r=k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-3q-r=n-k\\) horizontal lines, altogether \\(n\\) distinct lines. … Hence for any \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) a required configuration exists.”\n\n**Issue 9 – Overall construction invalid (Critical Error).**  \nBecause the building blocks (the “translates of \\(T_3\\)” and Lemma 4) are incorrect, the claimed covering of all points of \\(T_n\\) for arbitrary \\(k\\) is not established. The argument collapses.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Lemma 4\n\n> “The three sunny lines  \n> \\[\n> \\ell_1: y=2x-1,\\qquad\n> \\ell_2: y=3x-2,\\qquad\n> \\ell_3: y=5x-4\n> \\]  \n> contain respectively the points \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\) of \\(T_3\\). … Hence \\(T_3\\) can be covered by three sunny lines.”\n\n**Issue 10 – Lemma 4 does not cover the full set \\(T_3\\) (Critical Error).**  \nAs noted, the true \\(T_3\\) has six points; the three lines listed cover only three of them. The lemma’s claim that they “cover \\(T_3\\)” is false.\n\n---\n\n### 6. Completion of the Proof\n\n> “Lemma 3 shows that for every \\(n\\ge3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) a configuration exists.  \n> Lemma 2 proves that a configuration with \\(k=n\\) is impossible for all \\(n\\ge4\\).  \n> Lemma 4 (together with the explicit construction for \\(k=2\\) when \\(n=3\\)) shows that \\(k=n\\) **is** possible when \\(n=3\\).  \n> Consequently the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly \\(\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) for every \\(n\\ge3\\), and additionally \\(k=n\\) when \\(n=3\\).”\n\n**Issue 11 – Final answer not justified (Critical Error).**  \nThe conclusions rely on Lemmas 2, 3, 4, all of which contain critical errors as detailed above. Consequently the claimed set of admissible \\(k\\) is not proven; in particular the existence of configurations for many values of \\(k\\) (e.g., \\(k=2\\) for \\(n\\ge4\\)) remains unestablished.\n\n---\n\n### 7. Overall Assessment\n\nThe solution correctly identifies the counting of points in \\(T_n\\) and provides valid constructions for the extreme cases \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\). However, the core of the argument—Lemma 1’s bound on points per sunny line, Lemma 2’s impossibility for \\(k=n\\) (when \\(n\\ge4\\)), Lemma 3’s general construction for arbitrary \\(k\\), and Lemma 4’s covering of \\(T_3\\)—contains multiple **critical errors**:\n\n* Mis‑stated definition of sunny lines.\n* Unjustified claim that \\(\\Delta\\neq0\\) for all sunny lines.\n* Incorrect identification of the extremal line \\(y=x\\).\n* Invalid use of the faulty Lemma 1 in Lemma 2.\n* Faulty constructions for \\(k=2\\) when \\(n=3\\) and for general \\(k\\) (based on an incorrect description of \\(T_3\\)).\n* Lemma 4 does not actually cover the required set of points.\n\nBecause these errors break the logical chain, the final answer is not established. Hence the solution is **invalid**."
