\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   points of \\(T_n\\).  \n   By Lemma 1 each sunny line contributes at most \\(M_n\\) points, so the total number of\n   distinct points that can be covered is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   H_{n-k}+kM_n .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Using (2) and the explicit value of \\(M_n\\) we obtain\n\n   *if \\(k\\) is even, say \\(k=2t\\):*  \n\n   \\[\n   H_{n-2t}+2tM_n\n   =\\frac{n(n+1)}2-t\n   <N_n .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the required covering is impossible when \\(k\\) is even and \\(k\\ge2\\).\n\n4. **Lemma 3 (Odd \\(k\\ge5\\) is impossible).**  \n   Let \\(k=2t+1\\ge5\\) (so \\(t\\ge2\\)).  \n   As in Lemma 2 we first use the horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-k\\); they cover the points\n   described by (2).  \n   The set of points that remain uncovered is exactly the translate  \n\n   \\[\n   T_k'=\\{(a',b')\\mid a',b'\\ge1,\\;a'+b'\\le k+1\\},\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. a copy of \\(T_k\\) shifted by \\((0,n-k)\\).  Its cardinality is  \n\n   \\[\n   |T_k'|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   All remaining \\(k\\) lines must be sunny and have to cover the whole set \\(T_k'\\).\n   Applying Lemma 1 with \\(n\\) replaced by \\(k\\) shows that a sunny line can contain at most  \n\n   \\[\n   M_k=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor=t+1\n   \\]\n\n   points of \\(T_k'\\).  Because of (4) we must have equality in every instance, i.e. each of the\n   \\(k\\) sunny lines must contain exactly \\(t+1\\) points of \\(T_k'\\).\n\n   **Characterisation of the extremal lines.**  \n   Let a sunny line \\(\\ell\\) meet \\(T_k'\\) in \\(t+1\\) points.  By Lemma 1 this forces \\(|\\Delta|=1\\);\n   consequently the direction vector of \\(\\ell\\) is \\((B,-A)\\) with \\(|B-A|=1\\).  The only way to obtain\n   \\(t+1\\) points inside the triangle \\(T_k'\\) is to have \\(A=-B\\) (so the slope is \\(1\\)).  Hence every\n   extremal line is of the form  \n\n   \\[\n   y = x + c\\qquad(c\\in\\mathbb Z).\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Two distinct lines of the family (5) intersect in at most one lattice point, and inside the\n   triangle \\(T_k'\\) the numbers of points they contain are respectively  \n\n   \\[\n   t+1,\\;t,\\;t-1,\\dots ,2,1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Therefore the total number of points covered by any collection of \\(k=2t+1\\) distinct lines of\n   type (5) is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   (t+1)+t+(t-1)+\\dots +2+1\n   =\\frac{(t+1)(t+2)}2\n   <\\frac{(2t+1)(t+1)}2\n   =\\frac{k(k+1)}2 ,\n   \\]\n\n   the strict inequality holding for every \\(t\\ge2\\) (i.e. for every odd \\(k\\ge5\\)).\n   Consequently the set \\(T_k'\\) cannot be covered by \\(k\\) sunny lines, and a configuration with\n   an odd number \\(k\\ge5\\) of sunny lines does not exist.\n\n5. **Lemma 4 (Existence for \\(k=0,1,3\\)).**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  Take the \\(n\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n\\).  None of them is sunny and every\n   point of \\(T_n\\) lies on exactly one of them.\n\n   *\\(k=1\\).*  Use the \\(n-1\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-1\\); they cover all points of\n   \\(T_n\\) except the single point \\((1,n)\\).  Add any sunny line through \\((1,n)\\) (for instance\n   \\(y=2x-1\\)).  Exactly one line is sunny and all points are covered.\n\n   *\\(k=3\\).*  Keep the \\(n-3\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-3\\).  The points that are not yet\n   covered form the translate of \\(T_3\\) consisting of  \n\n   \\[\n   \\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge n-2,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   The three sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_1:\\;y=2x-1,\\qquad\n   \\ell_2:\\;y=3x-2,\\qquad\n   \\ell_3:\\;y=5x-4\n   \\]\n\n   (the three lines used for the case \\(n=3\\)) contain respectively the points\n   \\((1,n-2),(2,n-3)\\), \\((1,n-1)\\), and \\((1,n)\\) of this translate; after shifting them\n   upward by \\(n-3\\) units in the \\(y\\)-direction they become three distinct sunny lines that\n   together cover the whole translate.  Hence we obtain a configuration with exactly three sunny\n   lines.\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   Lemma 2 excludes every even \\(k\\ge2\\); Lemma 3 excludes every odd \\(k\\ge5\\); Lemma 4 shows that the\n   remaining values \\(k=0,1,3\\) are attainable for every \\(n\\ge3\\).  Therefore the complete set of\n   admissible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,3\\}} .\n   \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad N_n:=|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor to the line\n\\(x+y=0\\); equivalently a line written as \\(Ax+By=C\\) with integers \\(A,B\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\nA\\neq0,\\;B\\neq0,\\;A\\neq B .\n\\tag{S}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Maximum number of points of \\(T_n\\) on a sunny line)\n\nWrite a sunny line as \\(Ax+By=C\\) with \\(\\gcd(A,B)=1\\) and satisfying (S).  \nAll integer points on the line are\n\n\\[\n(a,b)=(a_0,b_0)+t\\,(B,-A),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb Z .\n\\]\n\nHence the sum \\(a+b\\) changes by  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta:=B-A\\neq0 .\n\\]\n\nInside \\(T_n\\) we have \\(2\\le a+b\\le n+1\\); therefore the number of admissible values of\n\\(t\\) is at most  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{(n+1)-2}{|\\Delta|}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\n   =\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{|\\Delta|}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\n   \\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nIf equality holds in (1) then \\(|\\Delta|=1\\).  Because \\(\\gcd(A,B)=1\\) and \\(|B-A|=1\\),\nthe only possibility is \\((A,B)=( -1,1)\\) (or the opposite sign), which gives the line\n\\(y=x\\).  Consequently a sunny line contains at most  \n\n\\[\nM_n:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(T_n\\), and the bound is attained only by the line \\(y=x\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Even numbers of sunny lines are impossible)\n\nAssume a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, where \\(k\\) is even and \\(k\\ge2\\).\nReplace the non‑sunny lines by the horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\ny=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=n-k .\n\\]\n\nThese \\(n-k\\) lines are distinct, non‑sunny and cover\n\n\\[\nH_{n-k}= \\sum_{b=1}^{\\,n-k}(n+1-b)\n        =\\frac{(n-k)(n+k+1)}2\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(T_n\\).\n\nBy Lemma 1 each sunny line contributes at most \\(M_n\\) points, so the total number of\ndistinct points that can be covered is at most  \n\n\\[\nH_{n-k}+kM_n .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nWrite \\(k=2t\\;(t\\ge1)\\).  Using (2) and the explicit value of \\(M_n\\) we obtain\n\n\\[\nH_{n-2t}+2tM_n\n   =\\frac{n(n+1)}2-t\n   =N_n-t<N_n .\n\\]\n\nThus the required covering of all \\(N_n\\) points is impossible when \\(k\\) is even and\n\\(k\\ge2\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Odd numbers of sunny lines \\(\\boldsymbol{k\\ge5}\\) are impossible)\n\nLet \\(k=2t+1\\ge5\\) (so \\(t\\ge2\\)).  \nAgain we first use the horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-k\\); they cover the points counted in\n(2).  The set of points that remain uncovered is the translate\n\n\\[\nT_k'=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge n-k+1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n      =\\{(a',b')\\mid a',b'\\ge1,\\;a'+b'\\le k+1\\},\n\\]\n\ni.e. a copy of \\(T_k\\) shifted upward by \\(n-k\\).  Its cardinality is\n\n\\[\n|T_k'|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nAll remaining \\(k\\) lines must be sunny and have to cover the whole set \\(T_k'\\).\nApplying Lemma 1 with \\(n\\) replaced by \\(k\\) shows that a sunny line can contain at most  \n\n\\[\nM_k=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor=t+1\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(T_k'\\).  Because of (4) we must have equality for every line; consequently each\nsunny line contains exactly \\(t+1\\) points of \\(T_k'\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.1  Geometry of a line attaining the bound\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be a sunny line meeting \\(T_k'\\) in \\(t+1\\) points.\nFrom Lemma 1 we know that \\(|\\Delta|=|B-A|=1\\).  Since the direction vector of \\(\\ell\\) is\n\\((B,-A)\\), the condition \\(|B-A|=1\\) together with \\(\\gcd(A,B)=1\\) forces \\(A=-B\\); otherwise\nthe step \\((B,-A)\\) would change the sum \\(a+b\\) by \\(\\pm1\\) while moving one coordinate by at\nleast \\(2\\), which would leave the triangle after fewer than \\(t+1\\) steps.  Hence every\nextremal line has slope \\(1\\) and can be written as  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_c:\\;y=x+c\\qquad(c\\in\\mathbb Z).\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nInside the triangle \\(T_k'\\) the line \\(\\ell_c\\) contains exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\#\\bigl(\\ell_c\\cap T_k'\\bigr)=\n\\begin{cases}\nt+1 &\\text{if }c=0,\\\\[2pt]\nt   &\\text{if }c=1,\\\\\nt-1 &\\text{if }c=2,\\\\\n\\vdots\\\\\n1   &\\text{if }c=t .\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThus the only way to obtain \\(t+1\\) points on a sunny line is to take the line \\(\\ell_0\\)\n(i.e. \\(y=x\\)); any other line of the family (5) meets the triangle in fewer points.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.2  Counting the points covered by \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines\n\nBecause the \\(k\\) sunny lines must be distinct, at most one of them can be \\(\\ell_0\\); the\nremaining \\(k-1=2t\\) lines are of the form \\(\\ell_c\\) with \\(c\\ge1\\).  Using (6) we obtain an\nupper bound for the total number of points of \\(T_k'\\) that can be covered:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\bigl|\\ell_0\\cap T_k'\\bigr|\n   &+ \\sum_{c=1}^{t} \\bigl|\\ell_c\\cap T_k'\\bigr|\n   \\le (t+1) + \\bigl(t + (t-1)+\\dots +1\\bigr)  \\\\\n   &= (t+1)+\\frac{t(t+1)}2\n   = \\frac{(t+1)(t+2)}2 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(t\\ge2\\) (i.e. \\(k\\ge5\\))\n\n\\[\n\\frac{(t+1)(t+2)}2\n   < \\frac{(2t+1)(t+1)}2\n   = \\frac{k(k+1)}2 = |T_k'| ,\n\\]\n\nso the \\(k\\) sunny lines cannot cover all points of \\(T_k'\\).  Hence a configuration with an\nodd number \\(k\\ge5\\) of sunny lines does not exist. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4 (Existence for the three remaining values)\n\n*\\(k=0\\).*  \nThe \\(n\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n\\) are all non‑sunny and together contain every\npoint of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*\\(k=1\\).*  \nTake the \\(n-1\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-1\\); they miss only the point \\((1,n)\\).\nAdd any sunny line through \\((1,n)\\) (for instance \\(y=2x-1\\)).  Exactly one line is sunny\nand all points are covered.\n\n*\\(k=3\\).*  \nKeep the \\(n-3\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-3\\).  The uncovered points form the set  \n\n\\[\nT_3'=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge n-2,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich is a translate of \\(T_3\\).  The three sunny lines\n\n\\[\n\\ell_1:\\;y=2x-1,\\qquad\n\\ell_2:\\;y=3x-2,\\qquad\n\\ell_3:\\;y=5x-4\n\\]\n\ncontain respectively the points \\((1,n-2),(2,n-3)\\), \\((1,n-1)\\), and \\((1,n)\\) of \\(T_3'\\);\nafter the translation they are three distinct sunny lines covering the whole set\n\\(T_3'\\).  Hence we obtain a configuration with exactly three sunny lines.\n\nThus for every \\(n\\ge3\\) the values \\(k=0,1,3\\) are attainable. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final conclusion\n\nLemmas 2 and 3 exclude every integer \\(k\\) except \\(0,1,3\\); Lemma 4 shows that those three\nvalues are indeed attainable for every \\(n\\ge3\\).  Consequently the set of admissible\nnumbers of sunny lines is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,3\\}} .\n\\]"
