red is at most  \n> \\[\n> H_{n-k}+kM_n .\n> \\tag{3}\n> \\]  \n> Write \\(k=2t\\;(t\\ge1)\\).  Using (2) and the explicit value of \\(M_n\\) we obtain  \n> \\[\n> H_{n-2t}+2tM_n\n>    =\\frac{n(n+1)}2-t\n>    =N_n-t<N_n .\n> \\]\u201d\n\n**Error 3 \u2013 Miscalculation of the total.**  \nThe algebraic simplification claimed is incorrect.  Compute directly:\n\n\\[\nH_{n-2t}= \\frac{(n-2t)(n+2t+1)}2\n        = \\frac{n^2+n-4t^2-2t}{2}\n        = N_n - (2t^2+t).\n\\]\n\nAdding \\(2tM_n\\) (with \\(M_n=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\)) gives\n\n\\[\nH_{n-2t}+2tM_n = N_n - (2t^2+t) + 2tM_n.\n\\]\n\nFor odd \\(n\\), \\(M_n=\\frac{n+1}{2}\\) and the expression becomes \\(N_n + t(n-2t)\\), **not** \\(N_n-t\\).  For even \\(n\\) the discrepancy is similar.  A concrete counter\u2011example: \\(n=5,\\;t=1\\) yields \\(H_{3}+2M_5 = 12+6 = 18\\), whereas \\(N_5-t = 14\\).  Hence the equality claimed is false.  **Critical Error.**\n\n**Error 4 \u2013 Invalid impossibility conclusion.**  \nThe statement \u201cThus the required covering of all \\(N_n\\) points is impossible when \\(k\\) is even and \\(k\\ge2\\)\u201d rests on the erroneous bound from Lemma\u202f1 and the faulty arithmetic above.  Since the bound is not established, the conclusion is unsupported.  **Critical Error.**\n\n---\n\n### 4. Lemma\u202f3 \u2013 Odd numbers of sunny lines \\(k\\ge5\\) are impossible\n\n> \u201cLet \\(k=2t+1\\ge5\\) (so \\(t\\ge2\\)). \u2026  \n> All remaining \\(k\\) lines must be sunny and have to cover the whole set \\(T_k'\\).  \n> Applying Lemma\u202f1 with \\(n\\) replaced by \\(k\\) shows that a sunny line can contain at most  \n> \\[\n> M_k=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor=t+1\n> \\]  \n> points of \\(T_k'\\). \u2026\u201d\n\n**Error 5 \u2013 Reliance on false Lemma\u202f1.**  \nBecause Lemma\u202f1 does not correctly bound the number of points a sunny line can contain, the derived bound \\(M_k=t+1\\) is unjustified.  Consequently the subsequent counting argument is baseless.  **Critical Error.**\n\n**Error 6 \u2013 Incorrect geometric claim.**  \nThe proof asserts that \u201cthe condition \\(|B-A|=1\\) together with \\(\\gcd(A,B)=1\\) forces \\(A=-B\\)\u201d.  As noted earlier, many coprime pairs satisfy \\(|B-A|=1\\) without having opposite signs (e.g. \\((A,B)=(2,3)\\)).  Hence the deduction is false, and the description of \u201cextremal lines\u201d as only those of the form \\(y=x+c\\) is unsupported.  **Critical Error.**\n\nSince the entire Lemma\u202f3 collapses, the claim that all odd \\(k\\ge5\\) are impossible is not proved.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Lemma\u202f4 \u2013 Existence for the three remaining values\n\n#### (a) \\(k=0\\)\n\n> \u201cThe \\(n\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n\\) are all non\u2011sunny and together contain every point of \\(T_n\\).\u201d\n\n*Correct.*  Horizontal lines are non\u2011sunny and clearly cover all points because the maximal \\(b\\)\u2011coordinate in \\(T_n\\) is \\(n\\).\n\n#### (b) \\(k=1\\)\n\n> \u201cTake the \\(n-1\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-1\\); they miss only the point \\((1,n)\\).  \n> Add any sunny line through \\((1,n)\\) (for instance \\(y=2x-1\\)).\u201d\n\n**Error 7 \u2013 Wrong example of a sunny line.**  \nThe line \\(y=2x-1\\) passes through \\((1,1)\\), not through \\((1,n)\\) for \\(n\\ge3\\).  Thus the provided example is factually incorrect.  The statement \u201cany sunny line through \\((1,n)\\)\u201d is true (e.g. \\(y=x+(n-1)\\) works), but the specific line cited does **not** satisfy the requirement.  This factual mistake is a **Critical Error** because the construction as written fails.\n\n#### (c) \\(k=3\\)\n\n> \u201cKeep the \\(n-3\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-3\\). \u2026 The three sunny lines  \n> \\[\n> \\ell_1:\\;y=2x-1,\\qquad\n> \\ell_2:\\;y=3x-2,\\qquad\n> \\ell_3:\\;y=5x-4\n> \\]  \n> contain respectively the points \\((1,n-2),(2,n-3)\\), \\((1,n-1)\\), and \\((1,n)\\) of \\(T_3'\\); after the translation they are three distinct sunny lines covering the whole set \\(T_3'\\).\u201d\n\n**Error 8 \u2013 The listed lines do not cover the required points.**  \nNone of the three lines passes through \\((1,n-2)\\) (plugging \\(x=1\\) gives \\(y=1\\) for all three).  Likewise they miss \\((2,n-3)\\), \\((1,n-1)\\), and \\((1,n)\\).  Consequently the claim that these three sunny lines cover the entire translate \\(T_3'\\) is false.  This is a **Critical Error**; the construction does not achieve the desired covering.\n\nBecause the existence proofs for \\(k=1\\) and \\(k=3\\) are incorrect, the statement that these values are attainable is unsupported.\n\n---\n\n### 6. Final Conclusion\n\n> \u201cLemmas\u202f2 and\u202f3 exclude every integer \\(k\\) except \\(0,1,3\\); Lemma\u202f4 shows that those three values are indeed attainable for every \\(n\\ge3\\).  Consequently the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is \\(\\{0,1,3\\}\\).\u201d\n\nSince Lemma\u202f1 is false, Lemma\u202f2 and Lemma\u202f3 are invalid, and Lemma\u202f4\u2019s constructions contain critical errors, the final conclusion is not justified.  The answer \\(\\{0,1,3\\}\\) may be correct in truth, but the provided proof does **not** establish it rigorously.  Therefore the solution fails the verification criteria."
